									</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/captain-kirby" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3440103); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=3440103&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1606851823" alt="Captain Kirby" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=3440103)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/captain-kirby" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(3440103); return false;" >Captain Kirby</a></span> <span class="odate time_1606845315 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">01 Dec 2020 17:55</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-50742/per-page-discussions">Per Page Discussions / Per page discussions</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/scp-5618/comments/show">SCP-5618</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4833783">
									

<p>I feel like this piece couldn't decide between being about the characters, or about the foundation responding to a temporal collapse, and rather than take the best of both worlds ended up in a space where neither really shined.</p>
<p>The levity of the footnotes takes away from the gravity of the situation rather than emphasizing it. Like, I can appreciate that sort of &quot;well, it's the end of the world I guess&quot; tone, but it doesn't work well in contrast to a very rigid clinical tone. If you wanted this to be a piece that focusses more on the characters, I would want to give them more space to breathe and develop so we can feel actual attachment to them.</p>
<p>If this piece was more supposed to be about the foundation's reaction as a whole, I would do away with the footnotes and make the conprocs tell a more detailed and nuanced story of what the foundation considers valuable. Right now the conprocs feel a little bland in terms of &quot;things the foundation would do&quot;. It leaves a lot up to generalization and abstraction rather than zeroing on particularly evocative details.</p>
<p>I also really don't think the listpages was effective here. Like, you could've just let the article end on the first page and I think you would've gotten the point across. The refresh button and next page artificially lengthens the piece. While sure, the piece isn't that long to begin with, that does make any additional length much more noticeable. If you're going for short, you really need to make sure you're making every word count, and most of the words on the new page are just a rehash of a memo on the previous page.</p>
<p>Overall, I think if this wants to be about the end of the world, it would be better to decide what part of the end of the world you want to actually focus on.</p>
<p>-1</p>

							</div>

								
					
